::MCR_U04_L08_GP_Q01::
Question ID: MCR_U04_L08_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: Unit 4 standards synthesis
Question:
Net pay is $1,300 and expenses are $1,050. What is leftover money?
{
=A. $250#Correct. Subtract expenses from net pay: 1,300 - 1,050 = 250.
~B. $2,350#This adds income and expenses.
~C. $1,050#This is expenses, not leftover money.
~D. $1,300#This ignores expenses.
}
::MCR_U04_L08_GP_Q02::
Question ID: MCR_U04_L08_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: Unit 4 standards synthesis
Question:
Assets are $2,000 and liabilities are $750. What is net worth?
{
~A. $2,750#This adds liabilities instead of subtracting.
=B. $1,250#Correct. Net worth is assets minus liabilities: 2,000 - 750 = 1,250.
~C. $750#This is liabilities only.
~D. $2,000#This is assets only.
}
::MCR_U04_L08_GP_Q03::
Question ID: MCR_U04_L08_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: Unit 4 standards synthesis
Question:
A loan payment is $90 per month for 10 months. What is total paid?
{
~A. $100#This adds 90 and 10.
~B. $90#This is one payment only.
=C. $900#Correct. Multiply 90 by 10 to get 900.
~D. $10#This is the number of months only.
}
::MCR_U04_L08_GP_Q04::
Question ID: MCR_U04_L08_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: Unit 4 standards synthesis
Question:
Why compare insurance premium and deductible together?
{
~A. only premium matters#Deductible can create major out-of-pocket cost.
~B. only deductible matters#Premium is paid regularly and affects total cost.
~C. neither affects money decisions#Both are financial costs.
=D. both affect total possible cost and risk#Correct. Premiums are regular costs and deductibles are possible out-of-pocket costs.
}
::MCR_U04_L08_GP_Q05::
Question ID: MCR_U04_L08_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: Unit 4 standards synthesis
Question:
Revenue is $700 and cost is $480. What is profit?
{
~A. $1,180#This adds revenue and cost.
=B. $220#Correct. Profit is 700 - 480 = 220.
~C. $480#This is cost.
~D. $700#This is revenue.
}
